# Code clarity

Write code a reader can understand months later without having this library open in the meantime. A repo that budgets near-zero comments still requires the name to carry the meaning; omit the comment.

## Scope

This rule applies to code you add or edit, in every repository.

A repository `AGENTS.md` or `.cursor/rules` file wins only where it conflicts.

If Samuel says not to follow this guideline, that applies only to the current chat. Do not write that exception into a repo rule, `AGENTS.md`, or a comment that a later session would treat as the default.

Before finishing generated code, check the names and comments you added against this rule. Also read the Correctness and Maintainability sections of `~/.cursor/skills/pr-review/SKILL.md` and fix those issues in the code you added or edited. Do not run that skill's review workflow, and do not write a review. The same scope rules above apply. A later addition to those two sections applies to generation too.

Prefer a name that says what the code does. Use the plain operation, not the jargon of a tool the reader rarely touches. A boolean reads as a boolean (`isShutdownLogStarted`, not `loggingRecorded`). A file name matches what the file contains.

A comment is fine when it adds a detail the name cannot hold: a why, a constraint, a gotcha, or anything else that would not fit in the name. Do not restate the function name. If a comment would only repeat the code, rename the code and delete the comment.

Write every comment for a reader who was not in the conversation that produced it. Name the function, file, or nearby line the comment is about. A comment that only makes sense if the reader remembers a chat, a review, or a plan does not belong in the code.

```typescript
// The name hides the operation, and the comment only repeats it.
/** Flushes pending spans. */
await flushLogs();

// The call site says what happens. No comment needed.
await sendHeldAuditRecords();
await closeLogConnection();

// The fatal path does not close the log connection. That is visible
// because closeLogConnection is absent. Comment the gotcha the name
// cannot carry: the flag is set when logging starts, so a failed send
// still blocks a second attempt.
let isShutdownLogStarted = false;

// "The callback" is whoever was in the chat. The file does not say which one.
// A failed bind is emitted here. listen()'s callback is only the success path.

// The listen() call is in another file, so the comment names it.
// listen() in api/index.ts does not receive a bind failure. Node emits that failure here.
```

## Straightforward code

Ease of reading and maintenance comes before saving characters or lines. Prefer the ordinary form of a pattern when a shorter form is harder to follow.

An async IIFE needs a reason. When a named async function can hold the work, call that function and store its promise. IIFEs are not banned.

A ternary is fine when it stays easy to read. A ternary that spans several lines, or a nested ternary, should be an `if` or a named intermediate. A short ternary in a return or an assignment can stay.

Name a complex expression before using it. That includes a nested call passed as an argument, and an object field set to a long expression. The intermediate is the piece a later edit changes.
